<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Clovis point</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Clovis points</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a> points are the fluted stone projectile points of the Clovis culture of prehistoric <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, all dated to roughly 13,400 to 12,700 years ago. A typical point is a lance-shaped blade about 10 centimetres long, shaped by careful pressure flaking, with a concave base from which grooves, called flutes, were removed on one or both faces so that it could be hafted. They are found in dense concentrations across much of North America. They are named after Clovis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>, where examples were first found in 1929. Whether they tipped thrusting spears, thrown darts or were also used for butchering is debated.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/clovis-point.jpg" alt="Clovis point" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daderot · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clovis_point,_11500-9000_BC,_Sevier_County,_Utah,_chert_-_Natural_History_Museum_of_Utah_-_DSC07376.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a> point is lenticular or nearly oval in cross-section and usually widest at or just below the middle, which often gives it a long sharp tip. Most are 75 to 110 mm long, 25 to 50 mm wide and 5 to 10 mm thick, and weigh roughly 25 to 35 grams. The flutes usually run 30% to 50% of the point's length, and the lower edges and base are ground smooth. Points were made of flint, chert, jasper, chalcedony and other stone of conchoidal fracture; those of quartz crystal tend to be smaller.</p><p>Few points survive in their original condition: most were reworked to resharpen or repair them, which can make their tradition hard to identify. They vary even at a single site, the eight found at Naco ranging from 2 to 4 inches. Around 10,000 years before present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a> points disappeared from the continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the Folsom point, mostly shorter and with longer flutes, took their place.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Of the around 6000 points classified as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, most were found east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, especially in the Southeast, though some researchers think many eastern points are misclassified. Finds reach, less commonly, as far south as Venezuela, and since sea level is now about 50 meters higher than in the Paleoindian period, coastal sites may lie under water. Major sites include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blackwater-draw">Blackwater Draw</a> type site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>, the Anzick site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montana">Montana</a> and the Gault site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>. Fraudulent points, some with false documentation, have also reached the market.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a> points have been identified in over two dozen caches with other stone and bone or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> tools, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a> to the Rocky Mountains. Most seem to be material stored at strategic places on the Pleistocene landscape, though the Anzick cache accompanied a child burial.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Whether the technology arose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, in response to the hunting of megafauna, or came from elsewhere is an open question. No antecedents have been found in northeast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, the homeland of the first Americans in the current consensus. The Solutrean hypothesis, drawing on similarities with points of the Solutrean culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, holds that hunters crossed the Atlantic ice-shelf, but it is not well accepted: the two technologies are distinct, Indigenous North Americans show no genetic evidence of European ancestry, and the route was likely unsuitable.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Clovis point” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The caches themselves, over twenty of them across North America, are collections of artefacts, usually stone tools such as <b>Clovis points</b> or bifaces, deliberately left in one place.</blockquote><a href="/culture/clovis-culture">Clovis culture · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
